  "body": "## What this changes\n\nIn `SecretKeyWrapper`, the app wraps a secret key with the public key and unwraps a blob as an AES secret key with the private key. RVSec reported that `SecretKeyWrapper.unwrap` reinitializes the Java `Cipher` after wrapping on the missing-key-file path; its rule does not permit that order.\n\nI changed `wrap` and `unwrap` to create a fresh `Cipher` for each operation instead of sharing one. The transformation, keys, modes, and wrapped-key representation remain unchanged.\n\nI found this while running the app under RVSec, a runtime checker for how apps use the Java crypto and TLS APIs.\n\nThe project builds with this change (`assembleDebug`).\n\nI'm a Computer Engineering student at the University of Bras\u00edlia (UnB), working on a research project on Android app analysis. Happy to adjust this to the project's conventions.\n\n## How it was checked\n\n- [ ] Ran the app and exercised the change\n- [ ] Checked the light, dark and black themes if the change is visual\n- [ ] Added or updated a test where the change is testable\n\n## Commit subject prefix\n\nThe changelog is generated from commit subjects. Use a prefix so the entry lands in the right\nsection, or leave it off and it will be filed under Improvement:\n\n`feat:` new  \u00b7  `fix:` fix  \u00b7  `perf:` / `refactor:` / `build:` improvement  \u00b7  `docs:` / `chore:`\n/ `test:` / `ci:` left out  \u00b7  add `!` before the colon for a breaking change.",
